title: A two-bit register
mode: draw
chapter: Memory
check: behaves
parts: dff chip:my_mux
needs: my_mux
---- intro
One bit of memory with **load** was one multiplexer and one flip-flop. A **register** is a row of them, all sharing the same `load` and the same clock, holding a whole number.

When `load` is on, at the tick `q1 q0` takes `d1 d0`. Otherwise it keeps its number.
---- task
Build a two-bit register: `q1 q0` takes `d1 d0` at a tick while `load` is on, and keeps its value otherwise.
---- start
input d0 0 0
input d1 0 150
input load 0 280
clock clk 0 340
led q0 520 20
led q1 520 170
---- reference
input d0, d1, load
clock clk
led q0, q1
wire a[1], b[1]
a = dff((a & ~load) | (d0 & load), clk)
b = dff((b & ~load) | (d1 & load), clk)
q0 = a
q1 = b
---- answer
input d0 0 0
input d1 0 150
input load 0 280
clock clk 0 340
chip m0 my_mux 160 10
dff f0 320 20
chip m1 my_mux 160 160
dff f1 320 170
led q0 520 20
led q1 520 170
f0.q -> m0.a
d0 -> m0.b
load -> m0.s
m0.y -> f0.d
clk -> f0.clk
f0.q -> q0
f1.q -> m1.a
d1 -> m1.b
load -> m1.s
m1.y -> f1.d
clk -> f1.clk
f1.q -> q1
---- hint1
Build bit 0 just as in the last lesson: my_mux into a flip-flop, the flip-flop's `q` back into the mux's `a`.
---- hint2
Then the same again for bit 1. Both muxes get `load` as `s`, and both flip-flops get `clk`.
---- solution
Each bit is a my_mux and a flip-flop: `q` back into `a`, `d` into `b`, `load` into `s`. The two bits share `load` and `clk`, so they load together.
---- done
Make it eight bits wide and you have a byte of memory. A processor's registers are exactly this, 32 or 64 bits wide.
